<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP, on purpose (0.17.2).

  Android has forbidden cleartext by default since API 28, and it is right to.
  This app is the case the rule does not fit: it talks to one server, on the
  user's own LAN, at an address that is an IP literal — 192.168.1.5 — and there
  is no certificate authority on earth that will issue for that. The choice is
  cleartext on a local network or a self-signed certificate the user has to
  install as a trusted root on their phone, which is a materially worse thing
  to teach somebody to do.

  What makes it acceptable is what the design already refuses: nothing here
  reaches outside the LAN. No relay, no tunnel, no account. The traffic crosses
  the user's own router and nothing else.

  Narrowed as far as Android allows. `domain-config` matches hostnames, not
  address ranges, so "private ranges only" is not expressible — this is why the
  base config is the one carrying the permission.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
